WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.240
Matthew Perry hoped that his "Friends" legacy&nbsp;
wouldn't be the main thing people remembered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.840
about him, and his final interviews hold some&nbsp;
haunting insights into why that was the case.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.280
People around the world were shocked and&nbsp;
heartbroken to learn that "Friends" star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:19.280
Matthew Perry had died at the age of 54 on&nbsp;
Saturday, October 28. In the days since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.320
social media has been flooded with touching&nbsp;
tributes to Perry, some of which have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.720
written by his colleagues and those who knew him,&nbsp;
others of which have been written simply by fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.880
of his work. What's been truly incredible,&nbsp;
though, is that Perry's iconic performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.760
as Chandler Bing on "Friends" hasn't been the&nbsp;
main focus of many of the tributes to him.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.840
Instead, many have gone out of their way&nbsp;
to acknowledge his real-life struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.000
with addiction and honor how candidly&nbsp;
he spoke about his life in his final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.560
years. People have partly been able&nbsp;
to do so because of the interviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:52.400
that Perry gave during the 2022 press&nbsp;
tour for his memoir, "Friends, Lovers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.240
and the Big Terrible Thing." While promoting that&nbsp;
book, Perry spoke with astonishing frankness and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.920
bravery about his difficult journey toward&nbsp;
sobriety, reflecting on the ups and downs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.560
his life and offering advice to anyone else&nbsp;
who might have ever felt lost or hopeless.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.760
Matthew Perry's press tour for "Friends, Lovers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.880
and the Big Terrible Thing" was defined by the&nbsp;
actor's unflinching honesty. In a sit-down he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.760
did for Macmillan imprint Flatiron Books to&nbsp;
promote his memoir, he got especially candid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:24.680
about his beliefs that positive change can come&nbsp;
to anyone, regardless of their circumstances.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.560
"There's a famous kind of line that people don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:33.560
change. I happen to know that people&nbsp;
do change and I see that every day."

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.960
The moving, contemplative candor&nbsp;
of that interview was present in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.680
many of the final public comments&nbsp;
that Perry made prior to his death.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.800
In an interview with People, Perry&nbsp;
explained why he hoped to use his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.520
sobriety as an avenue to help anyone who might&nbsp;
be struggling with the same issues he did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.000
and why he waited as long as he did, saying:

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.520
"I had to wait until I was pretty&nbsp;
safely sober — and away from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:59.080
active disease of alcoholism and addiction&nbsp;
— to write it all down. The main thing was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.520
I was pretty certain that it would help people."

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.680
The actor brought not only real&nbsp;
frankness to his later years but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.400
also a level of hard-fought&nbsp;
wisdom that made the weight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.760
of his struggles just as apparent as the&nbsp;
impressiveness of his personal victories.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.720
During an appearance on "Real Time with Bill&nbsp;
Maher," Perry further reflected on his life.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.720
"Reality is an acquired taste. That's what&nbsp;
I believe. I have had a great deal of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.280
a great deal of problems acquiring it."

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.560
It takes real courage for someone as&nbsp;
well-known as Perry to be as frank about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.960
his personal struggles as the actor was in many&nbsp;
of his last interviews. The former "Friends"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.360
star even went so far as to admit that he'd&nbsp;
come to view his personal highs more fondly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.200
than any of his professional accomplishments. He&nbsp;
didn't expect that to ever be the case, either.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:47.280
In what may have been Perry's final interview,&nbsp;
he recalled during a December 2022 appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.279
on "The Jennifer Hudson Show" that as&nbsp;
a young man, he was driven by fame.

00:02:51.279 --> 00:02:51.360
"I said, 'God, you can do anything you&nbsp;
want to me, just please make me famous.'"

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.600
He went on to say:

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.800
"About three weeks later, I got 'Friends,'&nbsp;
but God did not forget the other part and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.160
gave me a lot of tough stuff in my&nbsp;
life. That's how I realized that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.040
outside stuff doesn't really matter.&nbsp;
Being famous doesn't really matter."

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.440
In the wake of that powerful realization, Perry&nbsp;
revealed that he didn't want anyone to remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.440
him best as an actor, but as a survivor and ally&nbsp;
to others who have struggled with addiction.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.840
He had also said that during yet another candid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.520
conversation in November 2022&nbsp;
on "Q with Tom Power," saying:

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.880
"The best thing about me, bar none, is&nbsp;
if somebody comes up to me and says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.880
'I can't stop drinking. Can you help me?'&nbsp;
I can say, 'Yes,' and follow up and do it."

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.640
"When I die, I don't want 'Friends' to be the&nbsp;
first thing that's mentioned. I want that to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.760
be the first thing that's mentioned, and I'm&nbsp;
going to live the rest of my life proving that."

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.080
These are heartbreaking words to hear now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.280
but it looks like Perry built exactly&nbsp;
the legacy for himself that he wanted.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.160
If you or anyone you know needs&nbsp;
help with addiction issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.920
help is available. Visit the Substance Abuse&nbsp;
and Mental Health Services Administration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:06.434
website or contact SAMHSA's National&nbsp;
Helpline at 1-800-662-HELP (4357).

